# Frontend environment variables
#
# All are NEXT_PUBLIC_*, so they are inlined into the client bundle at BUILD
# time, not read at runtime. Changing any of them requires a rebuild, and none
# may hold a secret.

# Base URL of the backend API. No trailing slash, and no `/api` — request paths
# already include it (e.g. `/api/auth/google`).
# In production this is the deployed backend's public URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Public origin of this site, used for `metadataBase` — share-card and canonical
# URLs resolve against it. Left unset, they silently point at localhost, which is
# only visible once someone shares a link.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# -- Google sign-in -----------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Sign-in is Google-only: without this the sign-in page renders an
# error instead of a button and nobody can get in, including admins. Set it
# before deploying, not after.
#
# Public by design. A client ID identifies the app to Google and travels in every
# sign-in request; it is not a secret and belongs in the bundle. What stops
# anyone else using it is the authorised-origins list on Google's side, plus the
# backend refusing any token whose audience is not this ID. The client *secret*
# is a different value -- this app never uses it, so never set it here.
#
# Setup, once, by the team lead (see documents/google_sign_in_setup.md):
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com -> create or pick a project
#   2. APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen
#      - User type: Internal if dau.ac.in is the project's Workspace,
#        otherwise External
#      - Scopes: the default email, profile and openid. Nothing more is needed,
#        and anything more would trigger Google's verification review.
#   3. APIs & Services > Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID
#      - Application type: Web application
#      - Authorised JavaScript origins:
#          http://localhost:3000
#          https://<your-vercel-domain>
#        (origins only -- no paths, no trailing slash)
#      - Authorised redirect URIs: none. This app uses the ID-token flow, which
#        does not redirect.
#   4. Copy the Client ID below, and set the same value as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID on
#      the backend. They must match or every sign-in is refused.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ── Cloudinary (Phase 2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every image in Phase 2 — profile avatars, event photos, batch galleries — is
# uploaded browser-side through the Cloudinary Upload Widget. The browser posts
# the file straight to Cloudinary and the backend only ever stores the returned
# URL string, so no image binary passes through Spring or PostgreSQL.
#
# The backend therefore needs NO Cloudinary credentials at all. Both values here
# are public by design and safe in the client bundle — neither is an API secret.
# Never put the Cloudinary API secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable.
#
# Setup, once, by the team lead:
#   1. Create a free account at https://cloudinary.com
#   2. Cloud name is on the dashboard — copy it below.
#   3. Settings > Upload > Upload presets > Add upload preset
#      - Signing mode: Unsigned          (required: the widget has no secret)
#      - Folder: cpclub                  (confines uploads to one folder)
#      - Allowed formats: jpg,png,webp   (rejects non-images)
#      - Max file size: 5 MB
#      Copy the preset NAME below.
#
# Note on unsigned presets: anyone who reads the JS bundle can upload to this
# preset. The folder, format, and size limits above are what keep that bounded,
# so do not remove them. Rotate the preset if it is ever abused.
# There are deliberately no fallback values. If either is unset, the upload
# widget does not open and admins are offered a paste-a-URL box instead -- the
# code used to fall back to one contributor's personal cloud name, which sent
# the club's photos into somebody else's account without any error.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET=
